## Day 2

Day 2 was the first day of taking notes and learning the main concepts
of IAM. Authentication is the process of identifying whether or not a
user, machine, or whatever is requesting access is who they claim to be.
Authorization is the process of granting or denying access based on
whether they are who they say they are. Identity claims contain things
like the username and password of the entity trying to gain access.
Tokens are given to the user once they've been authenticated and carry
identity claims and attributes so the system can make authorization
decisions later. Tokens are stored client-side, meaning the server
doesn't need to look anything up — it just reads what's inside the
token. Sessions are different — they're created server-side right after
authentication and the server checks authorization every time the entity
tries to do something, confirming whether they're allowed to do what
they're attempting. Access decisions are the process that determines who
has access and what they have access to. Authn and Authz are kept
separate because it increases security — they work hand in hand to
provide proper security measures.

## Day 3

RBAC, or role-based access control, is a system that controls who gets
access to what based on their role. Roles are essentially titles that
entities fall under and each role has a certain amount of privilege in
the system. Permissions are the pieces inside each role that determine
what the people assigned to that role are allowed to do and what
resources they have access to. Role assignment is the process of
assigning roles based on what the entity needs to complete their task
and nothing more. Role explosion is when too many roles are created from
trying to make roles too specific for different tasks.

## Day 4

ABAC, or attribute-based access control, is a similar system to RBAC
but grants access in a different way. ABAC assigns and grants access
based on subjects, objects, actions, and environment attributes.
Subjects are the entities that want access, objects are the resources
they want access to, actions are what the subject is trying to do with
the object — whether that's read, write, delete, or execute — and
environment attributes are things like the time of day or where the
entity is requesting access from. Policy engines are the software that
evaluate all the attributes against the defined rules and produce a
permit or deny decision. XACML is the industry standard language for
writing those rules. PEP is what grants or denies the entity access
based on what PDP tells it. PDP requests and receives information from
PIP to make the decision of whether it should tell PEP to grant or deny
access. PAP is where security teams write and manage the access
policies. ABAC beats RBAC when access needs to be granted based on more
nuanced criteria than just roles.

## Day 5

The principle of least privilege is the idea that entities should only
have access to what they need and nothing more. Standing access grants
an entity access to privileged resources at all times, even when they
don't need it. Just-in-time access grants access only when a user needs
it and removes it when they don't need it anymore. Over-provisioning is
the number one IAM risk because if an account gets compromised and has
access to more than it needs, it gives the attacker more leeway to
control or view resources they shouldn't have and potentially escalate
the situation through lateral movement. PAM is essentially least
privilege enforced specifically on the highest-risk accounts, such as
admins and service accounts.

